Remark: 
In physics and engineering, a negative sign is often introduced in the definition of a potential function (i.e. so that a potential function g for a vector field F is one such that F = -∇g). 
The motivation behind such a convention is that in physical applications, it is desirable to have the potential function represent potential enegery in some sencse. 
For example, in the case of the gravitational field F = -(GMm/||r||²)u, a physicist would take the potential function to be -GMm/||r||, not +GMm/||r|| as we do. 
The advantage to the physicist in doing so is that the physicist's potential function increaes with increasing ||r||. 
The corresponds to the notion that the greater the distance between two bodies, the greater should be the stroed gravitiational potential energy. 

From Theorem 6.4 of Chapter 2 we know that the gradient of any C¹ scalar-valued function f:X ⊆ R'' -> R is perpendicular to the level sets of f. 
Thus, if F is a gradient vector field on R'', F(x) must be perpendicular to the level set of a potential function of F containing the point x. 
If f as such potential function, the level set {x | f(x) = c} is called an equipotential set (or equipotential surface if n = 3, or equipotential line if n = 2) of the vector field F. 
